  "final_answer": "I have successfully retrieved the solution from WolframAlpha. The differential equation y''(z) + sin(y(z)) = 0 (which is the classic nonlinear pendulum equation) has been solved.\n\n## Solution from WolframAlpha\n\n**Input:** y''(z) + sin(y(z)) = 0\n\n**Classification:** Second-order nonlinear ordinary differential equation, autonomous\n\n### Singular Solutions (微分方程奇异解):\n\nWolframAlpha gives two forms of the solution expressed using the **Jacobi amplitude function** am(x, m):\n\n$$y(z) = -2\\,\\text{am}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(c_1 + 2)(z + c_2)^2},\\ \\tfrac{4}{c_1 + 2}\\right)$$\n\n$$y(z) = +2\\,\\text{am}\\left(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{(c_1 + 2)(z + c_2)^2},\\ \\tfrac{4}{c_1 + 2}\\right)$$\n\nwhere:\n- **am(x, m)** is the **Jacobi amplitude function** (the inverse of the incomplete elliptic integral of the first kind)\n- **c₁** and **c₂** are arbitrary constants of integration\n- The argument **4/(c₁ + 2)** is the elliptic modulus parameter **m**\n\n### Additional Notes:\n- Equivalent form: y''(z) = -sin(y(z))\n- Complex-exponential form: y''(z) + ½i·e^(−iy(z)) − ½i·e^(iy(z)) = 0\n- WolframAlpha also displays a plot of an individual solution curve and a family of specific solution curves (sampled over y(0) and y'(0)).\n- A \"Step by step solutions\" option exists but requires Wolfram|Alpha Pro.\n\nThis ODE is the well-known nonlinear pendulum equation, whose general solutions naturally involve Jacobi elliptic functions (and reduce to trigonometric solutions when the amplitude parameter m → 0).",
